            <div xml:id="d403">

                <pb facs="0511" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">368.117/295: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Fisher</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-15T16:00:00+02:00">October 15, 1931—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 15—11:32 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>42. Department’s 30, September 30, 4 p.m. Greek Government will not engage itself
                    to exempt American citizens of Greek origin, naturalized since January 15, 1914
                    from military service during 1932. While informal exemptions may be made
                    especially for organized groups of excursionists it is preferable to omit last
                    paragraph of article No. 36 in Notice to Bearers of Passports.</p>
                <pb facs="0512" n="392" xml:id="pg392"/>
                <p>It may be advisable to include in new pamphlets reduced term of service and
                    payment as reported in Legation’s despatch 1840.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fisher</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
